        "assessment": "I checked every step against the target files (launch.json, tasks.json, scripts/start-debugger.sh, compose.yaml, orders/main.py, orders/http.py) and against base launch.json and main.py. The course reads correctly. It runs from F5 through preLaunchTask to the task, which runs the script on the host. The script checks that the container is running, installs debugpy if needed and fixes the log folder's owner, then starts debugpy detached. The walk then explains why localhost:5678 and /workspace reach the container, follows the attach with pathMappings, and ends with main() running in the container. W2 correctly follows the exit-1 branch (target start-debugger.sh 8–11) back to the editor. The claims I checked are true of the code: the base configuration was a launch of orders.main and was replaced; `problemMatcher: []` makes the task's exit the completion signal; the `if` around the command substitution keeps `set -e` from firing; the debugpy install lands outside the bind mount; `id -u`/`id -g` are expanded on the host; chown changes only the folder, so root-created files keep their own owner; `LOG_DIR.mkdir(exist_ok=True)` would create the folder anyway; `serve()` runs `serve_forever()` on port 8000. Points that depend on the editor or the platform (executable bit, attach timing, Debug Anyway, Docker Desktop ownership) are clearly marked as unverified rather than stated as fact. The first parts each run about two to three short sentences and give the step's role and where the walk goes next. W1.9 and W1.2 carry a little more detail than ideal (the list of what main() does; the env-var names), but nothing misleads and the extra is small. The deepenings build on the first parts rather than repeating them. Nothing false or significant falls short, so I accept.",
